\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia's Pres Urges Govt Action To Stabilize Rupiah  \b0\par\par\uc2 437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2005\par 04:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesian President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono called on his government Wednesday to cooperate with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to stabilize the rupiah after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a more-than-three-year low of IDR9,810 Tuesday against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "The president today instructed the vice president and ministers to take necessary actions to stabilize the rupiah to ensure it remains at a reasonable level, to communicate effectively and work closely with the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that its actions will be fruitful," a statement issued by the president's office said, without elaborating.  \par \par \uc2 In the statement, Yudhoyono also urged his government to reassure the public that Indonesia's finances, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, remain "strong" and that the rupiah is at a "reasonable value." [ 27-04-05 0236GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Indonesia recorded a $1.37 billion increase in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to $37.32 billion as of April 21 from a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Yudhoyono's statement marks the first time an Indonesian president has issued a public appeal for solidarity in the face of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations since the dying days of the regime of former dictator Suharto during the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 "The government calls on the public to remain calm...the economy is growing well and the political situation remains stable," the statement said.  \par \par \uc2 The statement reflects government efforts to head off any possible public panic over the slide in the value of the rupiah that might result in mass dollar purchases.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah responded to the sliding rupiah Tuesday by announcing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support plan that includes a pending interest rate hike of 15 to 20 basis points, the use of forward transactions to protect the rupiah and a move to "monitor" state-owned Pertamina's (PTM.YY) dollar purchases.  \par \par \uc2 The market rallied on the news and that, in tandem with Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by unloading what traders suspect was up to $150 million on the market, prompted a rebound in the rupiah's value with the U.S. dollar closing at IDR9,688 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Wednesday morning to support the rupiah, pushing the dollar to IDR9,665 at 0127 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts have cautioned that official efforts to stem the rupiah slide against the U.S. dollar won't address the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's basic problem of rising oil prices, which are powering massive dollar purchases by domestic state firms.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jakarta Bureau; Dow Jones Newswires; 62 21 3983 1277; dj.jakarta@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 27-04-05 0324GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050427e14r0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
