\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Bk Indonesia Rate Hike Unlikely To Boost Rupiah  \b0\par\par\uc2 947 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2005\par 14:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Phelim Kyne \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank Indonesia's plan to hike interest rates to support the tumbling value of the rupiah against the U.S. dollar will boost market sentiment but won't address the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's core problem of rising oil prices, which are fueling massive dollar purchases by domestic state firms, analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah Tuesday unveiled a rupiah defense plan that includes a pending increase in the benchmark Sertifikat Bank Indonesia, or SBI rate, of 15 to 20 basis points, the use of forward transactions to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a move to "monitor" state-owned Pertamina (PTM.YY)'s dollar purchases.  \par \par \uc2 That news and Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped spur a rebound in the rupiah's value with the U.S. dollar closing at IDR9,688 Tuesday, down from a-more-than three-year high of IDR9,810 earlier in the day and Monday's close of IDR9,750.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves are welcome, but they won't put much of a dent in the voracious domestic appetite for dollars powered by high oil prices, said JP Morgan Chase Bank's Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist, Claudio Piron.  \par \par \uc2 "The risk is we may see (the dollar) touch IDR10,000 by year's end," Piron said. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can watch Pertamina, but understand that Pertamina has a budgeted rate of $24 a barrel and outside that they have to go to the market to buy dollars (to purchase supply)."  \par \par \uc2 At 1025 GMT, the front-month June Brent contract on London's International Petroleum Exchange was down 41 cents at $53.99 a barrel in electronic trading, but above an intraday low of $53.88/bbl. Front month Nymex crude was trading 66 cents lower at $53.91/bbl.  \par \par \uc2 Piron said that Bank Indonesia had been "resting on its laurels a bit" in responding to the recent rupiah decline, but that movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflect a healthy managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shouldn't be cause for market gloom.  \par \par \uc2 "Generally speaking, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is responding to the macroeconomic environment...but (Bank Indonesia) has to get (support measures) just right because sentiment is fragile and if they overstep they may panic the market," he said.  \par \par \uc2 A weaker rupiah threatens the financial performance of local firms, which have substantial dollar debt, and could hurt Indonesia's most prolonged period of economic growth since the Asian financial crisis of 1997-98. Indonesia's government of President Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono has projected an average annual rise in gross domestic product of 6.6% from 2004 to 2009, outpacing 5.1% GDP growth last year.  \par \par \uc2 The rupiah's decline has also prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider introducing a new generation of money market management tools, including a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap instrument, to reduce U.S.-dollar buying by state-owned firms. Bank Indonesia's Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom told Dow Jones Newswires that the bank's board of governors would discuss the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap tool in a meeting Tuesday, without elaborating.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Interest-Rate Rise May Not Help Rupiah Long Term \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The psychological impact of Bank Indonesia's moves to support the rupiah will likely help keep the U.S. dollar at IDR9,500 to IDR9,600 for the next few weeks, but projections beyond that period remain "quite hazy," said ABN Amro Bank Asian sovereign and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist, Emmanuel Ng.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ng said that raising interest rates to absorb excess liquidity won't influence domestic companies' purchases of dollars to pay foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the market will have a positive spin on (the rupiah support measures) because it shows that Bank Indonesia is being proactive," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But raising interest rates will only have transitory impact," Ng said. Monitoring "Pertamina and the use of forward transactions would have the most impact because those two measures would actually address the real dollar (transactions)."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That view was echoed by Citibank economist Anton Gunawan, who said the current circumstances behind the decline in the rupiah mirrored those in May to July 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia helped stem the rupiah's slide against the dollar at that time by trimming the estimated $30 million that Pertamina spends daily on oil supplies. It did that by limiting those purchases to state-owned bank transactions closely monitored by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gunawan said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's efforts to bolster the value of the rupiah against the dollar may get a boost from a massive U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injection from an unlikely source: U.S. cigarette manufacturer Philip Morris.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philip Morris, a unit of the world's biggest tobacco company Altria Group Inc. (MO), said last month that it will pay $2 billion to buy a 40% stake in Jakarta-listed PT Hanjaya Mandal Sampoerna (HMSP.SK) from a group of major shareholders. Sampoerna is Indonesia's third-largest cigarette maker by sales.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Philip Morris will make a general offer for the rest of Sampoerna which, if fully accepted, will lift the value of the total acquisition to $5.2 billion, including around $160 million in debt.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupiah may benefit from an influx of U.S. dollars timed to meet an end-May payment deadline for a portion of the Sampoerna acquisition, Gunawan said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "(Philip Morris) has already brought in about a billion, probably...and if they bring some more money in then the rupiah value can go to IDR9,400 to IDR9,500, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Phelim Kyne of Dow Jones Newswires' Jakarta Bureau; 62 21 3983 1277; phelim.kyne@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited By Iain McDonald [ 26-04-05 1234GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050426e14q000o8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
