\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Ctrl Bk Intevenes To Stem IDR Slide: Dealers  \b0\par\par\uc2 360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2005\par 06:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 0425 GMT Tuesday to stem the slide in the value of the rupiah against the dollar, fueled by local firms' purchases of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hedge offshore obligations, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "The bank kept selling dollars at IDR9,802 to IDR9,785," a dealer with a state-owned bank told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounted to less than $20 million so far Tuesday, without elaborating. [ 26-04-05 0447GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said he expected Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to protect the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say a weaker rupiah could hurt the financial performance of local companies that have significant dollar debt, which in turn would hurt the nation's economy. The Indonesian government and private companies have around $130 billion in foreign debt.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar rose to IDR9,805 at 0345 GMT, topping its three-year high of IDR9,750 at Monday's close as local firms continued their dollar purchases before the end of the month, when their offshore obligations are due.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah is preparing to equip the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap instrument to reduce U.S. dollar buying by state-owned companies that he blamed for the recent weakness in the rupiah, the Bisnis Indonesia newspaper reported Tuesday. The report listed state petroleum firm PT Pertamina (PTM.YY) and electricity company PT Perusahaan Listrik Negara as examples of state-owned firms that are securing large amounts of U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that they need for up to the six months.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board of governors will discuss the need for swaps and other money market instruments later Tuesday, Bank Indonesia Senior Deputy Governor Miranda Gultom told Dow Jones Newswires, without providing a specific timetable for when the bank might implement their use.  \par \par \uc2 "We hope more instrument options could become the answer for the (dollar) supply-demand gap," Gultom said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Edhi Pranasidhi and I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires' Jakarta Bureau; dj.jakarta@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 26-04-05 0513GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050426e14q0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
