\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Indonesia c.banker says Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  to adjust slowly.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2004\par 18:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BERLIN, Nov 21 (Reuters) - A senior Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Sunday a Group of 20 call for exchange rate flexibility would lead to higher Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but the adjustment would be a slow one.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking to Reuters on the sidelines of a G20 meeting in Berlin, Halim Alamsyah, director of Economic Research and Monetary Policy at Bank Indonesia, also said Indonesia was happy with the current level of the rupiah while he warned the budget deficit target might not be achievable this year.  \par \par \uc2 G20 finance ministers and central bankers, closing a two-day summit, urged Asian economies to move towards "greater exchange rate flexibility".  \par \par \uc2 When asked whether flexibility in exchange rates would lead to higher exchange rates in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Alamsyah said "it's looking like that way. Large movements in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have a big impact on the Asian economies. I don't think the alignment of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be abrupt."  \par \par \uc2 "We don't want to see the dollar sliding too much, of course, but if the market mechanism is in place and if it is supported by the necessary adjustment within each country including the U.S., I think it will be okay," he said in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a nine-year low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, extending its recent sharp losses on concerns about the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The G20 call on flexibility appeared to be a reference to China, which came under international pressure to revalue its pegged yuan higher to help correct global imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 "I think there was a general feeling in the meeting that the global imbalance is not only between China and the U.S. or between Europe and the U.S. But it is the general concern of G20 members and will be discussed at the next meeting," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We have to rely on market mechanisms to remedy the global imbalances, but at the same time we are also looking to adopt a kind of self-initiative response - not only China but each relevant countries should make some adjustment."  \par \par \uc2 HAPPY WITH RUPIAH In the case of Indonesia, Alamsyah said possible depreciation of the rupiah - which is trading between 8,900 and 9,100 per dollar this month - could have a negative impact.  \par \par \uc2 "The depreciation of the rupiah and also in some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not have an expansionary impact on the economy. Instead, it may cause a contractionary impact," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He said \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a regular basis in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - was comfortable with the current level of the rupiah.  \par \par \uc2 Alamsyah said: "We are not focusing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate at a certain level. We try to reduce volatility because it creates uncertainty. This approach is being carried out by many Asian countries."  \par \par \uc2 "The current level of the rupiah, around 9,000, is okay for the purpose of promoting exports and maintaining public expectations on inflation and also for exports and the sustainability of our fiscal situation. We are quite happy."  \par \par \uc2 He also warned the government's budget deficit target might not be achievable. Indonesia has a budget deficit aim this year of 1.3 percent to gross domestic product. But this could be in jeopardy because of lower government revenue.  \par \par \uc2 "It may increase a bit this year to 1.5 percent but next year there is general agreement within our government that it should be and can be reduced to one percent," he said.  \par \par \uc2 For economic growth, it has targets of five percent this year and 5.5 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 "GDP targets are realistic and achievable of course. The most dynamic component of our economy is the investment ratio. The ratio this year will be around 20-21 percent and next year will be around 22-23 percent. That would be enough to drive the economy towards 5.5 percent (growth) next year," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "We have achieved macro economic stability with less inflation. Now we need investment from abroad as well as domestically... Seven percent annual growth is possible going forward if we increase the investment ratio to 27-28 percent within the next five years."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041121e0bl00b9l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
