\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won weaker as oil stays firm, yen lifts others.  \b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2004\par 11:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, Sept 29 (Reuters) - The Korean won slipped in\par offshore markets on Wednesday, catching up with the rest of the\par region after high oil prices exerted downward pressure on Asian\par stocks and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The benchmark global oil price stayed around $50 a barrel,\par just short of Tuesday's record high, but, despite the threat of\par those high energy costs hurting growth and earnings in Asia,\par the trend in regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was mixed.\par  \par     The yen was off Tuesday's six-week lows and that helped the\par Sing dollar and Thai baht post small gains.\par     Some of the positions were driven by expectations ahead of\par Japan's quarterly tankan corporate sentiment survey due on\par Friday and a Group of Seven meeting that begins on Oct. 1.\par     The yen has been sold recently because of worries that an\par improvement in Japanese business sentiment might have stalled,\par but it was bought back on Wednesday.\par     The Indonesian rupiah was slightly higher at 9,205/9,210 a\par dollar.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     But, when Taiwan's markets opened after Tuesday's holiday,\par the Taiwan dollar fell to a one-month trough of 34.10 and\par Taiwan stocks to a three-week low.\par     Korean markets were closed for a third day for the full\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 moon festival and the won was unmoved in onshore trade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Offshore markets initially pushed it to 1,157 a dollar, its  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 lowest in a month and down from Friday's levels around 1,149.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It later rallied to 1,153.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "It's a holiday in Korea and the tendency of offshore\par markets is to overshoot," said one Singapore-based trader.\par     "The street is looking at 10.4 on yen-won and so they are\par pushing the won lower."\par     Markets believe the South Korean authorities are anxious to\par maintain the won's competitiveness versus the yen in export\par markets.\par     This week, with Korean markets closed and the yen weakened,\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the cross rate hit 10.28 won per yen, the won's highest against  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the yen since October 2003. That cross moved back to 10.4 on  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Traders said the yen was probably heavily oversold and some\par people were buying the won to bet on a yen rally.\par     While high oil prices remained a concern, traders said\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region found some respite on Wednesday as oil\par stabilised near its peaks.\par     The baht trimmed some of its losses after falling on\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday, when Thailand said it had its first known probable  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 case of one human infecting another with bird flu. It hit a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 three-week low of 41.61 on Tuesday but was higher at 41.55 on  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The Philippine peso came further off Tuesday's low of\par 56.45, closing at 56.315.\par     It hit the record low of 56.45 for the third time since\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March on Tuesday and then rallied slightly on suspicion the  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had sold dollars. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  denied  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , operators were divided between buying the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rupiah to prepare for gains once Susilo Bambang Yudhoyono is  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 officially sworn in as president and announces his cabinet and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the likely impact on growth from Indonesia's position as a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 temporary oil importer.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0814 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.11         111.64       +0.48\par  Sing dlr        1.6938         1.6961       +0.14\par  Taiwan dlr      34.010         33.998       -0.04\par  Korean won     1153.00        1149.00       -0.35\par  Baht             41.53          41.61       +0.19\par  Peso             56.32          56.33       +0.03\par  Rupiah         9205.00        9230.00       +0.27\par  Rupee            46.09          46.07       -0.04\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.11         107.31       -3.42\par  Sing dlr        1.6938         1.6978       +0.24\par  Taiwan dlr      34.010         33.978       -0.09\par  Korean won     1153.00        1192.00       +3.38\par  Baht             41.53          39.59       -4.68\par  Peso             56.32          55.49       -1.46\par  Rupiah         9205.00        8410.00       -8.64\par  Rupee            46.09          45.60       -1.07 ((Editing by\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alan Raybould; vidya.ranganathan@reuters.com; Reuters  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Messaging: vidya.ranganathan.reuters.com@reuters.net; +65 6870  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 3090)).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument