\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rate cut unsettles won.  \b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2004\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The South Korean won was\par unsettled by a surprise rate cut from the Bank of Korea on\par Thursday, which heightened worries about the economy while\par boosting the domestic stock market.\par  \par     Other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were moved little on Thursday,\par mimicking a stable yen and euro.\par     The won fell to 1,165 a dollar after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par caught markets off guard by cutting the overnight call rate\par target by 25 basis points to a record low of 3.5 percent.\par     It rallied slowly back to early levels around 1,157, even\par as markets debated the impact of the rate cut on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Bank of Korea said boosting domestic demand was a\par priority and higher oil prices were hurting growth.\par     "On first impulse, the market had to sell the won. This\par seems to have signalled the economy is in bad shape," said one\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist in Singapore.\par     Still, strategists differed on whether South Korea would\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 simultaneously encourage won gains to ward off pressures on the  \par \par \uc2 trade account and inflation from higher oil prices, or whether  \par \par \uc2 the Bank of Korea (BOK) had signalled growth would take  \par \par \uc2 priority.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     U.S. oil prices held near $45 a barrel, this week's record\par highs, and Korea is the world's fourth-largest importer of oil.\par     "The BOK seemed to admit that oil price rises were\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 disinflationary in the medium term. Later comments about export  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 growth likely to slow down to single digits suggest that they  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 are more worried about growth than inflation," Bhanu Baweja of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the authorities would not allow the won to depreciate  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 too much against the dollar, they would also probably prevent  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the won from gaining against the yen and Sing dollar, he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The yen-won cross was higher, with a yen yielding 10.47\par won, against 10.35 on Tuesday.\par     But Korean stocks rose 1.8 percent on Thursday, and have\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 been benefitting from heavy foreign portfolio investment flows  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 this month. Analysts said that would prop up the won despite a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 shrinking yield.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Funds flows have been more dominated by portfolio  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investment in equity as opposed to carry trade," Sun-Bae Kim of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs wrote in a note.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "On balance, an easier monetary policy stance may well\par prove won bullish by encouraging more equity portfolio inflow."\par     REST OF ASIA\par     Elsewhere, the U.S. dollar stabilised after Tuesday's rise\par in U.S. rates, helping the yen recover some ground.\par     The Singapore dollar was slightly higher and trading closer\par to 1.71 per dollar, compared with the previous day's lows near\par 1.7170.\par     The Taiwan dollar was a shade higher within its narrow\par ranges just on the weaker side of 34 per dollar.\par     The Thai baht was trading just shy of 41.57 per dollar, a\par one-year low it struck in offshore deals.\par     The Philippine peso rose to 55.58, its highest since June,\par while the Indonesian rupiah failed to benefit from the yen's\par rise and remained near the week's lows around 9,270 a dollar.\par     The rupiah has been volatile this week amid suspicion Bank\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its defence. Traders say the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 approaching presidential election in September, declining real  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 interest rates and the abundance of domestic cash are leading  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 to outflows from Indonesia.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0808 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       110.58         111.29       +0.64\par  Sing dlr        1.7118         1.7123       +0.03\par  Taiwan dlr      34.140         34.140       +0.00\par  Korean won     1157.30        1159.60       +0.20\par  Baht             41.51          41.50       -0.02\par  Peso             55.67          55.64       -0.04\par  Rupiah         9250.00        9245.00       -0.05\par  Rupee            46.26          46.30       +0.09\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       110.58         107.31       -2.96\par  Sing dlr        1.7118         1.6978       -0.82\par  Taiwan dlr      34.140         33.978       -0.47\par  Korean won     1157.30        1192.00       +3.00\par  Baht             41.51          39.59       -4.64\par  Peso             55.67          55.49       -0.31\par  Rupiah         9250.00        8410.00       -9.08\par  Rupee            46.26          45.60       -1.44.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040812e08c005sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngbl