\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Down but supported as Fed, oil weigh.  \b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2004\par 04:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, Aug 11 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell on\par Wednesday after the United States raised rates, but they were\par supported by a view that regional authorities would defend\par their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease the pressure from oil prices.\par  \par     The Japanese yen        fell more than 0.6 percent from\par Tuesday's levels and was quoted near 111.30/40 a dollar.\par     Barring the usually volatile Indonesian rupiah       , most\par of the Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lagged the yen.\par     The rupiah        was back at Monday's one-month lows near\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 9,250 a dollar despite the suspicion the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday and Bank  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stating it would keep domestic cash conditions  \par \par \uc2 tight.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar erased gains made on Tuesday on  \par \par \uc2 the back of unexpectedly strong GDP data. It fell back over a  \par \par \uc2 quarter of a percent to levels of 1.7160/70 a dollar.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Korean won             and Taiwan dollar\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 barely moved from the week's narrow ranges, overlooking the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 weaker yen as they found support in rallying stock markets and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the U.S. Federal Reserve's optimism on the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed raised rates by 25 basis points to 1.5 percent on  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday following a similar quarter point rise on June 30. The  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed said the economy had been hit by higher energy costs but  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 was poised for faster growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude oil prices hit fresh highs over $45 a barrel  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 on Tuesday, complicating the outlook for global growth and  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To the extent that global conditions for growth  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 deteriorate via higher oil prices, that should be bearish  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar," said Ray Farris, markets strategist with CSFB in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But higher oil prices would also be a negative factor for  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of the heavy oil-importing Asian economies, the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 only difference being that policy makers in Asia often used  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as policy tools, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Higher oil prices mean higher inflation in a region that  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 already has higher inflation, so it is difficult to allow  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to depreciate and import further inflation."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the global outlook for growth begins to deteriorate, it  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 will be unpalatable for Asia to appreciate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and it  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 will be unpalatable to increase interest rates," Farris said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That meant Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would not be allowed to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 depreciate, nor would any appreciation that hurt export  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 competitiveness be tolerated until the growth outlook improved,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Farris said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0214 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.38         110.62       -0.68\par  Sing dlr        1.7164         1.7121       -0.25\par  Taiwan dlr      34.157         34.098       -0.17\par  Korean won     1158.00        1155.00       -0.26\par  Baht             41.47          41.38       -0.22\par  Peso             55.65          55.66       +0.03\par  Rupiah         9255.00        9195.00       -0.65\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       111.38         107.31       -3.65\par  Sing dlr        1.7164         1.6978       -1.08\par  Taiwan dlr      34.157         33.978       -0.52\par  Korean won     1158.00        1192.00       +2.94\par  Baht             41.47          39.59       -4.55\par  Peso             55.65          55.49       -0.28\par  Rupiah         9255.00        8410.00       -9.13\par  Rupee            46.34          45.60       -1.61.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040811e08b002bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
