\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesian Rupiah Ends Up On Dlr Pft-Taking, Ctrl Bk Move   \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2004\par 12:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--The Indonesian rupiah closed higher Monday as foreign market participants took profit against the dollar and Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  allegedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the local unit, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at 9,340 rupiah, down from its close Friday at IDR9,465.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers said foreign players took profit against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after its slide against the yen to around Y110 spurred dollar-selling across Asia.   \par \par \uc2 Taking advantage of the dollar profit-taking, Bank Indonesia allegedly sold the unit through state banks, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 The nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly said it would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help the rupiah, which last week fell to a 26-month low of IDR9,590 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Ebbing demand from local companies also paved the way for the dollar's slide, dealers said. They added that the usual large dollar buyers, such as state-owned oil and gas company Pertamina, were not heard bidding for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Local companies were the main dollar buyers recently as they were hedging their offshore debts amid concerns of rising political tension ahead of the presidential election July 5.   \par \par \uc2 Choosing a president, in the country's first direct election for the post, could take up much of the rest of the year - creating a long period of uncertainty, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers, however, said that ebbing dollar demand could be just because local companies are waiting to buy the dollar at lower levels.   \par \par \uc2 'Dollar demand from local companies may resurface near IDR9,300,' a dealer with a foreign bank said.   \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect the dollar to trade between IDR9,310 and IDR9,360 Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 -By I Made Sentana,Dow Jones Newswires; 6221-3983-1277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Shishir Mudgil   \par \par \uc2 -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020040607e06700899\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
