\par\par \b \uc2 Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Dep Gov: Still  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  To Help Rupiah  \b0\par\par\uc2 351 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2004\par 04:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Wednesday it was continuing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah but ruled out hiking interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a 26-month low in early trade against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "We will continue to be active in the market, and will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it's necessary," Aslim Tadjuddin, a Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor, told Dow Jones Newswires. [ 02-06-04 0231GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling dollars over the past couple of weeks to slow the rupiah's decline, but to little avail. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen 9% since the start of May, and is trading at IDR9,485 to the dollar early Wednesday, weaker than IDR9,380 at Tuesday's close.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are concerned over Indonesia's presidential elections July 5, which many believe could lead to a long period of political fighting. Campaigning for the elections began Tuesday, sparking a new round of dollar buying.  \par \par \uc2 "Of course elections are a factor," Aslim said.  \par \par \uc2 Fears of rising global interest rates, especially a U.S. rate hike, have also increased the risk of investing in emerging markets such as Indonesia, analysts say. Higher rates increase the cost of borrowing, and make emerging markets less attractive.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say Bank Indonesia needs to increase interest rates to help support the rupiah, and nip growing inflationary pressure in the bud.  \par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned "increasing interest rates could hurt demand," Aslim said. Indonesia's annual 5% economic growth depends largely on consumer spending at a time when investment remains weak.  \par \par \uc2 Local companies have been buying dollars strongly amid concerns the U.S unit could fall further, making foreign debt and imports more costly.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors that poured money into Indonesian stocks and bonds last year have also been pulling out their money. Foreigners pulled out about IDR2 trillion from the government bond market in April and May, Aslim said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Dow Jones Newswires; 6221 3983 1277; tom.wright@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Brett Miller [ 02-06-04 0258GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040602e0620001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
