\par\par \b \uc2 Bk Indonesia: Rupiah Comfort Zone IDR8,200-IDR8,700/Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 308 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 08:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA (Dow Jones)--Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be happy to see the dollar trading between IDR8,200 and IDR8,700 for the rest of the year, its Governor Burhanuddin Abdullah said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The rupiah comfort zone is between IDR8,200 and IDR8,700," Burhanuddin told reporters. "Bank Indonesia won't take any action if the rupiah fluctuates within the range."  \par \par \uc2 This is the first time in recent months when officials from the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have mentioned a desirable trading level for the local unit  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at IDR8,365 at 0710 GMT, down from its close Wednesday at IDR8,385.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia floated the rupiah in late 1997 as the Asian financial crisis hit \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as and when it feels necessary.  \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia's Burhanuddin said he expects the benchmark weighted-average rate on the Sertifikat Bank Indonesia notes, or SBI, to be between 8.5% and 9% by the end of the year, compared with 8.59% now.  \par \par \uc2 His forecast is in line with most economists' forecasts amid the softer inflation outlook this year.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists even expect the SBI rate to fall to 8% on the assumption inflation will fall to 6% in 2003 from above 10% last year.  \par \par \uc2 On-year inflation fell to 6.2% in September from 6.38% in August.  \par \par \uc2 Another highlight for the economy is that Moody's Investors Service Tuesday upgraded Indonesia's sovereign rating by one notch to B2, saying falling public debt, and rising foreign reserves, mean the country is unlikely to default on its foreign borrowings.  \par \par \uc2 -By Linda Silaen, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; linda.silaen@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kevin Lim & Simon Harvey  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Simon Harvey  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031002dza20007l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
