\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah Ends Up As Ctrl Bk Warns Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2003\par 12:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- The Indonesian rupiah rebounded against the dollar in volatile trading Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary, amid continued concerns stemming from Tuesday's bomb blast in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  The dollar ended at 8,600 rupiah, down from its previous close of IDR8,665 but still above Monday's closing level of IDR8,490. \par \par \uc2  The dollar shot up to an intraday high of IDR8,710 in thin trading early in the session on follow-through buying from Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But the dollar came off its intraday high, falling to an intraday low of IDR8,550 before the lunch break after Bank Indonesia Deputy Governor Aslim Tajudin said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is committed to guarding the rupiah and expects the local unit to recover soon. \par \par \uc2  Dealers, however, said Bank Indonesia didn't appear to be selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Indonesian stocks rebounded 1.2% to 494.436 points on bargain hunting in blue chip shares after falling 3% Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said that the blast outside the Jakarta Marriott Hotel, which killed at least 10 people and injured around 150 others, will damp confidence in the Indonesian economy, at least in the short term. \par \par \uc2  The slump in direct foreign investment, which started during the 1997-98 Asian financial crisis, will remain protracted because of the latest bombing, said Anton Gunawan, a Jakarta-based economist with Citigroup. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said sentiment on the rupiah remains bearish as the latest blast shows the difficulties President Megawati Sukarnoputri's government is facing in its efforts to fight terrorism. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that the market remains jittery as the first verdict on a key suspect involved in the Bali bombing will be delivered Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The police have said there are similarities between the Bali bombing and Tuesday's attack, although it hasn't been confirmed that both attacks were carried out by the Jemaah Islamiyah terrorist group. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah is expected to remain volatile Thursday with the dollar expected to trade between IDR8,550 and IDR8,700, traders said. \par \par \uc2  -By I Made Sentana, Dow Jones Newswires; 62-21 39831277; I-Made.Sentana@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Kevin Lim \par \par \uc2  1159 1848 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030806dz86000q2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
