\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST-Indonesian Business News - July 25. \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2003\par 03:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Following are the leading business stories in the main Indonesian newspapers on July 25. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy. \par \par \uc2  Telephone: Editorial, (6221) 384-6364. Fax (6221) 344-8404 or Help Desk, (6221) 344-8406. Fax: (6221) 381-0862 \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  BISNIS INDONESIA \par \par \uc2  - INDONESIA LOSES 50 TRLN RPH A YR IN GOVT PROJECT TENDERS \par \par \uc2  The anti monopoly watchdog said one of its studies showed Indonesia suffers losses of 50 trillion rupiah ($5.8 billion) a year due in tenders for government projects due to unfair practices. \par \par \uc2  - IBRA APPOINTS TRIMEGAH (TRIM.JK) TO SELL INDOSIAR (IDSR.JK) \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's Bank Restructuring Agency (IBRA) has appointed Trimegah Securities to help sell its remaining 8.26 percent stake in the country's leading broadcaster PT Indosiar. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  KOMPAS \par \par \uc2  - GOVERNMENT PLANS TO CUT GAS PRICES FOR FERTILIZER FIRMS \par \par \uc2  The government plans to cut gas prices for fertilizer firms from Friday to $1 per million metric British thermal unit from $1.30. \par \par \uc2  - GOVERNMENT TO OPEN NEW PADDY FIELDS IN EIGHT PROVINCES \par \par \uc2  The agriculture ministry plans to open new paddy fields in eight provinces across the country to replace other planting areas hurt by the drought in a bid to overcome a potential rice shortage. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  THE JAKARTA POST \par \par \uc2  - RUPIAH HITS NEW LOW AS PRESSURE CONTINUES \par \par \uc2  Pressure on the rupiah  continued on Thursday, sending the local unit to a new multi-month low early in the day but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped mitigate the losses. \par \par \uc2  - BPUI REPAYS TIMAH (TINS.JK) WITH A PLOT OF LAND \par \par \uc2  After a five-year dispute, state-owned mining company PT Timah has agreed for PT Bahana Pembinaan Usaha Indonesia (BPUI) to settle its $17 million and 61 billion rupiah debt owed to the Tin miner through a debt-to-asset swap. Under that agreement Bahana will surrender a 1.76 million square metre plot of land. \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  EKONOMI NERACA \par \par \uc2  - BULOG ASKED TO STOP RICE IMPORTS THIS YEAR \par \par \uc2  The Agriculture ministry asked state logistics firm Bulog to stop rice imports this year because national unhusked rice production, estimated to reach 53 million tonnes, is enough to cover domestic demand. \par \par \uc2  - RUPIAH IS STILL IN A SAFE RANGE \par \par \uc2  Chief economics minister Dorodjatun Kuntjoro-Jakti and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's senior deputy governor, Anwar Nasution, played down the recent fall in the rupiah saying the swings were acceptable for a floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  - - - - \par \par \uc2  ($1=8,580 rupiah) \par \par \uc2  Looking for more information from local sources? For details, please call your local help desk . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030725dz7p0038s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
